+### Library numerics below the tiers
+
+PyTorch enables two approximations by default, underneath the whole tier
+system: `torch.backends.cudnn.allow_tf32` truncates fp32 convolution inputs to
+10 mantissa bits, and
+`torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_bf16_reduced_precision_reduction` lets a bf16
+GEMM accumulate split-K partials below fp32. Both change what is computed, so
+by the definitions above they belong to `high`.
+
+They are process-global and a server mixes tiers across concurrent batches, so
+they cannot follow a request. The runtime sets both explicitly per worker and
+logs the result, instead of inheriting whatever the installed PyTorch defaults
+to:
+
+| Flag | Default | Effect of turning it off |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `--allow-cudnn-tf32` | `true` (PyTorch's own default) | fp32 convolutions run on fp32 CUDA cores instead of TF32 tensor cores. Affects the VAE decoders that default to `vae_precision="fp32"` |
+| `--allow-bf16-reduced-precision-reduction` | `true` (PyTorch's own default) | bf16 GEMMs accumulate split-K partials at fp32 |
+
+The defaults keep PyTorch's values so that `exact` is no slower than it was
+before these flags existed. A deployment that needs the strict contract — fp32
+and bf16 math at reference precision throughout — passes both as `false` and
+accepts the slower convolutions.
